    "conclusion": "Nominally identical questions do not show meaningful instability at the semantic-class level in the available exact-repeat data: all 16 anchors remained semantically stable across five repetitions. Some broader response-state variation may occur, but conclusions about exact wording, referents, abstention, and evidence sets require caution because several corresponding measures had only moderate, weak, or poor reliability. Paraphrasing appears to alter components of response state—raising full-state distance for 13 of 16 anchors and affecting claim state, action, evidence, uncertainty, assertion, referent, and abstention/request behavior—without changing semantic class. Thus, explanations based on pervasive semantic randomness under identical conditions should be rejected, while wording-sensitive changes in nonsemantic response features should be retained. Relevant verification may causally correct errors whereas irrelevant verification does not, but this explanation remains provisional because only 2 of 24 trajectories were eligible."
